  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 327 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nAs of 230 AM Monday, a highly amplified midlevel ridge remains \nacross the central US and is flanked by troughs on each coast. A \nremnant outflow boundary from thunderstorm activity across western \nMO Sunday evening continues to progress southwest and currently \nextends from Lyons to Wichita to Arkansas City. Regional radar \nreveals a complex of thunderstorms moving across northern NE and SD \nwithin a broad zone of 850-700 mb WAA. Unlike the past few mornings, \nthermodynamic profiles are not as favorable for isolated \nthunderstorms as the LLJ veers near dawn. Similar to Sunday \nafternoon, a weak low-level baroclinic zone across eastern KS\nthis afternoon will overlap a moist airmass comprised of PW \nvalues up to 1.75\". This may yield a few isolated thunderstorms\n(mainly east of Highway 77) with MLCAPE up to 1000 J/kg of \nMLCAPE. Effective shear will remain 20-25 kt, supporting mainly\npulse convection. DCAPE up to 1300 J/kg may support localized \ndownbursts near 60 mph. In regards to temperatures, with the \nridge axis overhead, afternoon highs near 100 degrees are \nexpected area-wide through Tuesday. The Heat Advisory remains in\neffect through Tuesday evening. \n\nMid to long range guidance are trending towards an end of the long \nstretch of unseasonably hot temperatures. The initial pattern change \nbegins late Tuesday into Wednesday as a midlevel shortwave trough \nejects across the northern Plains, shunting a surface cold front \ninto the state. Exact timing of the front will be pivotal for high \ntemperatures Wednesday afternoon. Areas ahead of the front are \nlikely to surge near 100 degrees with post-frontal areas in the 80s \nor low 90s. The frontal zone should sweep across the entire forecast \narea by Thursday with high temperatures in the 80s for most \nlocations. As the frontal zone progresses across the area, shower \nand thunderstorm chances will increase as well. PW values may exceed \n2\" near and ahead of the front, suggesting heavy rainfall is \npossible. \n\nTransitioning into Thursday night and Friday, zonal midlevel flow \nwill become established across northern half of CONUS with the next \nshortwave trough emerging across the Pacific northwest. The initial \ncold front will slide well-southeast of the area with the next \nsurface trough developing across the high Plains. This will return \nsoutherly flow to the forecast area. WAA driven showers and \nthunderstorms are possible late Thursday night and Friday morning. \nTemperatures will surge back into the 90s areawide Friday afternoon \nahead of the next cold front. This cold front will sweep across\nthe area Saturday with additional shower/thunderstorm chances \nas well. This frontal passage will similar to Wednesday's with \nsoutherly flow quickly returning Sunday into Monday as another \nlee surface trough develops across the high Plains. The frontal \npassage that many Kansans have been long awaiting looks to \nsweep across the area during the Monday/Tuesday timeframe of \nnext week. A stout 1025 mb post-frontal surface high will usher\nin a taste of fall!",
